Date: Wed, 22 Sep 93 10:29:17 CDT
From: eder@hsvaic.boeing.com (Dani Eder)
Subject: SPACE: limits on launch costs

Re: Tethers

Dr. Forward is a good man, but he is not on the leading edge of
large structure research.  Herewith follows a summary of recent 
concepts in this area:

Prior Concepts:

The original idea for a 'beanstalk', or 'space bridge' involved
a cable hanging down from synchronous orbit to a spot on the
Earth's equator, with additional mass above synchronous orbit as
a counter-balance so the center of gravity is at synchronous
altitude.  Since the cable would orbit in 24 hours, and the Earth
rotates in 24 hours, the cable would appear to hang motionless over
a spot on the ground, and all you have to do is ride an elevator]
or some other simple system to get into space.

Now, consider the bottom end of the cable, just above the equator,
with an elevator cab massing 1 ton hanging on it.  The cable must
be able to support this weight.  Go up 1 kilometer, and the cable
must support the elevator cab PLUS the weight of the 1 km of
cable below that point.  If you use the strongest available material
(Carbon fiber of 6.9 GPa (10^6 psi) tensile strength), you need
a cross section of 1.42 square millimeters to support 1 ton at
the Earth's surface.  Over a length of 1 kilometer, you then have
a volume of 1.42 liters of fiber.  This fiber has a density of
1.82 kg/liter, so you add 2.59 kg of cable to the load 1 km up.
Thus the cross sectional area must increase 0.26% to support the
added load.  Each sucessive km must increase by a similar amount,
if gravity were constant.

Gravity is an inverse square field.  If you integrate it to infinity,
you find the potential is the same as the surface gravity (1g) times
the radius of the planet (6378 km).  Thus, the cross sectional area
increase is (1.00259)^6378 = 14.5 million.  Synchronous orbit is not
at infinity, it is 97.7% up the gravity well, so the cross section
increase is 10 million.  Thus, at sunchronous altitude, the area
of the cable is 14.2 square meters, and you have 26 tons of cable
per meter of length.  Roughly, the cable will have an effective
length of 10,000 km (the actual length is 35,000 km from synchronous
orbit to the ground, but the cable is tapered).  Given the weight
per length, we have a total cable mass of 260 million tons.  If we
produced high strength carbon fiber in the quantities required to
build such a structure, it might cost as little as $10/kg, so we have
a materials cost of 2.6 trillion dollars.

Since we can only support one ton of payload, and it would take
a couple of hours for an elevator to climb high enough to not be
a structural impact, you might deliver 10 tons per day.  At
10% per year for amortization and maintenance, you spread the
total cost of the system over an effective 10 years of elevator
rides, or 36,500 tons of cargo.  Thus you have 7,100 times as
much cable mass as payload mass, and the cost/kg is $71,000,
which is a bit worse than rockets can achieve today.

Note also, that I assumed the maximum strength material with no
margin of safety and no allowance for support systems.  In a
real system, we might use 60% of the material strength.

Now, assume that we have material twice as strong as today's
best carbon fiber (2 million psi).  Now the taper per kilometer
drops by half, and the cross section increase goes to (1.0013)^6231 =
3,179:1.  By doubling the strength, we drop the cross section increase
to the square root.  If we work at 60% of maximum strength, we have
a cross section increase of 683,000:1, which is a factor of 14.6 reduction
from the fist case.  Hence the cost/kg would drop to under $5,000,
which is a significant improvement over today's launch costs.

Thus, it has been often stated that 'space bridges' require 2 million
psi materials or better to be feasible.

This is only true if you are not smart.

Current Concepts:

Several people proposed smaller structures that would not go all
the way to synchronous orbit.  The bottom of these structures would
move relative to the ground, but would still be easier than using
a rocket all the way to orbit.  Hans Moravec is one of these people.
Brian Tillotson at Boeing proposed 'multi-stage' structures.  I've
proposed large ground based components to these structures.  Combining
all this you get the following:

A 300 km tall tower built on the Earth's surface.  From the top
you play out a 600 km cable with the payload on the end.  The
cable is rotating around the top of the tower like a very big
version of a rock on the end of a string which you are whirling
over your head.  At 3 g's, the tip velocity is 4.2 km/s, and the
end rides at 100 km altitude, so there is not much drag.  A drive
motor at the top of the tower provides energy to spin the cable.

The cable masses 35 times as much as the payload, and the tower
masses 20 times as much as the cable, so the total structure is 
700 times the mass of a single payload.  Because you have much more
energy in the cable than in a single payload, you don't want to
stop the cable between launches.  Instead you let payloads crawl
out to the end of the cable and release.  If you have 5 payloads
crawling on the cable at a time, evenly spaced, you double the
total structural mass.  If it takes one hour for each payload 
to to move 120 km on the cable, then you can fire 1 payload/hr,
or 87,600 payloads over the effective life, while the structural
mass is 1400 payloads.  Thus the structure/payload mass ratio is
0.016.  Even at aircraft cost/kg, the tower contributes under
$15 to launch cost/kg.  At bulk fabricated composites cost ($300/kg)
You contribute under $5/kg to launch cost.

So, you have this payload flung at 4.2 km/s.  Assume it is due
east at the equator, and at 100 km altitude.  Orbital velocity
is 7.9 km/s, and the earth contributes 0.4 km/s from it's rotation,
so you need 3.3 km/s more velocity to get to orbit.  You could do
this with an on-board rocket.  In this case, you would have about
a 20% payload fraction, and you would have about 1 kg vehicle/kg
payload, and 3 kg of fuel.  Looking at the final useful payload in
orbit, you might have a total cost of $40/kg.

Alternatively, you could have a partial space bridge in orbit,
and the bottom end moves 3.3 km/s less than orbital speed. If
the space bridge is 4000 km long, and the bottom is at 100 km, then
the center of gravity will be at 2100 km altitude, and will orbit
at a speed of 6857 m/s.  The bottom end, moving in a shorter path,
but at the same angular rate as the center of the bridge, moves
6478/8478 = 76.4% as fast, or 5240 m/s.  Since we got 4240 m/s
from the flinger and 464 m/s from the Earth's rotation, we have
a deficit of 532 m/s.  Allowing for mismatch in release time
from the ground, which will affect the direction you are thrown, and
allowing for some maneuvering capability, we will allow 750 m/s
of total maneuvering capacity to the vehicle.  

The net payload delivered in this case is 60% of the vehicle
mass.  The average g force on the length of the bridge is 0.3, 
so the taper is (1.00130)^2000 = 13:1.  The effective length
is 2600 km times the maximum cross section.  For a 1 ton payload,
the cable section is 2.36 square mm at the bottom, and thus 
31 square mm at the center.  Thus we have a total of 80.6 cubic
meters of cable, or 147 tons of cable per ton payload.  At
arrival, the vehicle masses 0.8 times it's start mass, of which
0.6 is the net payload, so we have another one third factor
in overhead.  Thus the orbital bridge will mass 200x the net
useful payload.  

Counting the ground tower and the orbital bridge, we have a
contribution of perhaps $10/kg from structure and $3 from the
vehicle, for a total of $13/kg to orbit.  And this system, since it
never goes above 3 g's, could carry people.  Assuming a 1 ton
payload, we require 1600 tons of structure, which at $300/kg
would cost $480 million.  Total development cost, including the
vehicle, should be around $1 billion.

Dani Eder



