Date: Wed, 22 Sep 93 11:01:19 CDT
From: eder@hsvaic.boeing.com (Dani Eder)
Subject: SPACE: limits on launch costs

Re: price drops and demand rises

If you can find me someone willing to risk a billion dollars on the 
hope that space launch traffic will dramatically rise, and willing
to wait the number of years it will take for the traffic to respond,
please do.  In the mean time, I'm concentrating on systems that
can make money by cutting launch costs within the current traffic
demands.  Then, if the traffic expands in response to the first
cost drop, you can go on to the next stage of improved system.

Another factor, in the real world, is the psychology of convincing
the people who control the puse strings.  I have a much greater
chance convincing my management to pursue a concept that has
jet engines, wings, and landing gear than I have something that
looks like an orbital bridge.  Thus I am working on a jet carrier
that drops a rocket at Mach 2.5 and 50,000 feet, and the rocket
goes on from there.  It looks a lot like a fighter dropping a
missile, so it appears to be a much less risky venture - we have
lots of engineers who know how to design such things, we know
where to get all the parts, etc.

True - it won't be the ultimate in low cost, efficient space
transport, but it should give an order of magnitude improvement
over what we have today.  And the development cost has to be
no more than a few hundred million dollars, so we can recoup
the development in no more than 50 flights or so over a 5 year
period, so the return on investment will be decent.  This means
no new propulsion development - we have to stick to off-the
shelf engines and as much as possible use off-the-shelf parts.

Dani

