Date: Wed, 22 Sep 93 11:44:29 -0700
From: kwatson@netcom.com (Kennita Watson)
Subject: PHIL: Recovering Objectivist

   A more general point: most people encounter Rand when they're in their 
   teens, and her ideas become part of their adolescent self-discovery, rebellion
   or whatever. It's sobering to realise that some of these people grow up 
   and raise their own kids in it!

Want something more sobering?  What about when they encounter Rand in
their teens, then raise their own kids _before_ they grow up?  As I
recall, my mom encountered Rand not long before I was born when she
was sixteen.  I can hardly believe it myself, and I _lived_ it.
"Kids, don't try this at home!" :-)

   In fairness to objectivism, I think the
   consequences depend more on the characters of the parents than on the
   content of the ideas ... sort of like if your parents had been Christians
   or socialists, what matters is whether they influence you in an 
   authoritarian or a libertarian manner, not whether the ideas are 'in
   themselves' for or against freedom in the abstract.

I think the ideas do matter.  Some ideas are expressed in terms of
absolutes, and some are not.  Rand's ideas are the former.  A or
not-A, good or evil, rational or irrational, "Any compromise between
good and evil is evil", etc.  As such, they run the risk of fomenting
a rigid mindset, if not an authoritarian one (especially in cases
where the mindset is not already, um, set).

I think Mom was a good enough Objectivist to realize that she couldn't
_force_ me to be rational, but the tacit "who would want to be
otherwise?" message was as powerful as that of any cult.  Actually,
the cult idea is worth study -- I do recall an atmosphere of "I may be
lonely, but at least I'll know I was doing the right thing.".  Any
Objectivism deprogrammers out there :-) ?

Kennita (somewhat surprised and gratified that people even seem interested)

