I've been reading these anti-Objectivisim comments (in this group,
that is) for a while. Frankly, I think the "dangers" of Objectivism
are being over-stated.

It's a lot of fun to poke fun at Rand and some of her screwy
idiosyncracies (smoking, cult of personality, "A is A" elevated to an
atomic priniciple of the universe, etc.). I've done it myself. (It
occurs to me that Rand never had a character who was evil because "He
knew truth, but he poked fun at it anyway." If she'd understood humor,
maybe she could've tried...)

Most of us read Rand, or Rand-influenced writers in the libertarian
belief system. Few of us turned into classic Randroids, though a few
have turned up recently.

Most of us skipped large sections of John Galt's speech and mainly
took from Rand a general notion that "we are not our brother's
keeper," that in fact such a philosophy of self-esteem and
self-interest is in fact a "good" thing.

When the competents folks of "Atlas Shrugged" withdrew their support
of the system (by vanishing to Galt's Gulch, withdrawing their
economic support, etc.), Rand was recognizing the important point that
the points about "apodictic self-exclusion" is secondary. What's
really important is that transactions with other people are
_voluntary_, that goods and services flow best in free markets.

This is the real lesson I took with me from Rand, and the lesson which
made things seem very clear. 

The talk of "recovering Objectivists" is cute, and fun, but is mostly
just a joke. 

Some folks like Rand, some don't. I read "Atlas Shrugged" in
1968...but have been unable to get past page 25 since. It's badly
written, in some sense, but is also powerful propaganda. As
propaganda, I put it on a par with Heinlein (like most of us, I'd read
all 40 or so novels he wrote by the time I was in the 9th grade). And
definitely superior to the current darling, L. Neil Smith.

So long as you don't take up smoking and wearing capes with a dollar
sign pin on your collar, and citing the "refutations of the
non-exclusionary corrolaries," there's little danger in Rand.


-Tim, Unreconstructed Rand Supporter (but who refuses to believe that
she had all the answers--an absurd idea--and is much more of an
anarchocapitalist than she was prepared to be)


