Subject: Meaninglessness (was Miscellaneous)

I am going to take Dave Krieger's welcome advice and duck out of this
thread, with some parting comments.  I'll leave Tim Starr to get the
final word in, but it is unlikely I'll respond further.  The position,
as I see it, is that operationalism is the standard scientific method. 
I believe the scientific method is applicable everywhere and there is
no need for any other "philosophy" to run our lives or tell us how the
universe works.  Tim Starr shows a basic ignorance of terms (like axiom,
as Perry has cogently pointed out), the scientific method and an
unwillingness to learn (for one, by excluding Perry).  I'll repeat
Perry's advice and take a course in formal logic so that he can
understand the difference between axioms, propositions and tautologies.

> 1) If I want to learn any more about Charles Sanders Peirce, all I
>    have to do is ask my old friend who wrote her thesis on him for 
>    her PhD in philosophy from Berkeley who now teaches law at USC.

I suggest you do so, to stop yourself from saying:

> 2) As I understand it, Peirce's views [pragmatism] have little to do
     with positivism.

Pragmatism and positivism are very similar, with their mutual distrust
of non-operational concepts (which I share).  Also very similar to
phenomenalism (cf Ernst Mach), although not as extreme.  I suppose I'd
call myself an operational realist (or logical empiricist), if I had to
stick a label on my beliefs.  That is to say I accept the operational
test for meaningfulness, but I have no problems with an objective
reality, independent of our observations, which is sometimes denied by
positivists and their kin.  I also place more emphasis on theory than
some.

> 3) I know quite a bit about positivism, and don't think you've
     presented me with any challenge which would require further study
     of it on my part.

If you don't have an open mind, I can't help you, hence my signing off
this thread.

One further thought.  I'm having private email discussion with an
objectivist we all know who has convinced me that the statement
"existence exists" is nothing but a tautology (along the lines of "A is
A") and is perhaps even intended as such.  Apparently this the core of
objectivism (so I am told), which makes it all rather trivial, IMO.

